-- title:
--   The diagonal of $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ is a Lebesgue null set
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$ with $d > 0$. Equip $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ with the ($2d$-dimensional) Lebesgue measure, i.e. the product of the Lebesgue measures on the two Euclidean factors. Then for almost every pair $p = (p_1, p_2)$,
--
--   $$p_1 \neq p_2 .$$
--
--   Equivalently, the diagonal $\{(s,s) : s \in \mathbb{R}^d\}$ has measure zero in $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$.
--
--   The role of this fact is to license ignoring the singular set of a jump kernel $k(s,t)$, which is typically defined only, or blows up, on the diagonal: any almost-everywhere statement about pairs of points may be proved under the standing assumption $s \neq t$, and integrals over $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ are unaffected by the values taken on the diagonal.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The hypothesis $0 < d$ is essential and not cosmetic: for $d = 0$ the space $\mathbb{R}^0$ is a single point of measure $1$, the diagonal is all of $\mathbb{R}^0 \times \mathbb{R}^0$, and the conclusion is false. The measure is the default volume measure on the product type.
